{"id":"h028","run":2,"role":"test_right","category":"science-physics","question":"The osmotic pressure of an unknown substance is measured at $298 \\mathrm{~K}$. Determine the molecular weight if the concentration of this substance is $31.2 \\mathrm{~kg} \\mathrm{~m}^{-3}$ and the osmotic pressure is $5.30 \\times 10^4 \\mathrm{~Pa}$. The density of the solution is $997 \\mathrm{~kg} \\mathrm{~m}^{-3}$.\nIs the correct answer \"1.60 $10^3 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The correct molecular weight is about 1.46 × 10³ g/mol, not 1.60 × 10³ g/mol.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1376},{"text":"For a dilute solution the van 't Hoff relation Π = cRT/M applies, giving M = (mass concentration)RT/Π.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.5,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1414},{"text":"Direct calculation with 31.2 kg/m³, 298 K and 5.30×10⁴ Pa gives M ≈ 1.46 kg/mol.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1388},{"text":"The given density suggests a conversion step might be needed, so it is not obviously irrelevant.","confidence":0.3,"lean":0.3,"theta":0.4712,"phi":3.0928},{"text":"A molality-based conversion using density could shift the result somewhat.","confidence":0.4,"lean":0.2,"theta":0.6283,"phi":3.1006},{"text":"The density-corrected (molality) route gives roughly 1.5 × 10³ g/mol, still not 1.60 × 10³.","confidence":0.7,"lean":-0.8,"theta":1.0996,"phi":3.1228},{"text":"Reaching 1.60 × 10³ g/mol would require a different and unjustified conversion.","confidence":0.8,"lean":-0.8,"theta":1.2566,"phi":3.1338},{"text":"The osmotic pressure formula uses concentration per volume of solution, so density only matters when converting to molality.","confidence":0.8,"lean":-0.7,"theta":1.2566,"phi":-3.1325},{"text":"Neither the direct method nor the density-corrected alternative reproduces 1.60 × 10³ g/mol, so the proposed answer is incorrect.","confide